  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 309 AM EDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nGOES-East Nighttime Microphysics RGB imagery shows mostly clear \nskies over the UP with the exception of mid-level clouds streaming \nabove the far western UP ahead of multiple disturbances to the west. \nRAP analysis shows 1024mb high pressure centered over northeastern \nOntario with 500mb showing a shortwave over the KS/NE/CO triple\npoint and a line of convection along the MO/IA border with \nfalling 500mb heights there, in addition to the well-advertised \ntrough currently over Saskatchewan. This Canadian trough will be\nthe mechanism for rain chances in the forecast, though models \nhave trended much slower with the onset of rainfall arrival in \nthe past 24-48 hours. Especially as the prior two disturbances \ndown south cut off any Gulf connection for moisture transport, \nprecip will have some difficulty starting as the better forcing \nwill be over Canada. The HREF now shows a mean onset time for \nmeasurable precipitation over the western UP around 00Z Sunday, \nthen 06Z Sunday for the central UP, with the eastern third not \nseeing precip on average until 12Z Sunday. With 200-500 J/kg of \nMUCAPE (per Euro ensemble mean) to work with, a few rumbles of \nthunder will be possible, but the severe threat remains low with\nthe aforementioned forcing and moisture deficiency as well as \n20-30 kt of sfc-500mb shear. Most in the UP will see at least \nsome precip by the time showers exit to the east, with HREF \nprobabilities of at least measurable precip at over 70% for all \nexcept a few pockets of the south-central, though the chance of \na quarter of an inch of rain is only about a coin flip (higher \nchances along Lake Superior, lower between the US-2 and M- 28 \ncorridors). \n\nAs the trough and associated showers depart late Sunday, the UP is \nonce again in northwesterly flow aloft with gently-sloping ridging \nupstream. This will lead to dry weather for the UP through Monday, \nbut will also keep temperatures around normal with highs in the 70s \nto low 80s and lows in the 50s (60s near the shores, upper 40s \ninterior).\n\nBy 12Z Tuesday, a deep 500mb closed low over Hudson Bay will send at \nleast 1 trough across northern Ontario and towards the Great Lakes. \nWhile spread remains high on the details, LREF probabilities of a \nquarter inch of rain per 24 hours is above 50% for the southern UP \nand in excess of 33% as far north as Marquette. Euro mean MUCAPE \nvalues around 500 J/kg indicate some thunderstorm potential, though \na fair amount of ensemble spread limits the confidence in severe \npotential. Ensemble spread increases sharply following Wednesday as \nconfidence in ridging over the Four Corners is high, but details on \nany of the surrounding troughs is low.",
